  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nUpper air analysis shows a large trough of low pressure centered \nover the northeast US with ridging over the western half of the \ncountry. Several shortwave disturbances are rotating through \nthe trough early this afternoon, with one of note working \nthrough central Illinois. This feature has provided enough low-\nlevel moisture and synoptic lift to result in scattered showers \nwhich are working into southeast parts of the state as of 1 pm. \nDrier conditions are expected through the overnight hours for \nmost with the exception being over east-central Illinois where a\nlake-effect rain/snow band is forecast to stretch as far south \nas a roughly Bloomington to Mattoon line. Overnight lows \nhovering around freezing will be cold enough to allow the first \nsnowflakes of the season to fall for some, with even some light \naccumulations of a tenth or two of an inch possible on grassy, \nelevated surfaces. Precipitation chances will linger into Friday\nbefore the fetch over the lake turns more north-northwesterly, \nshifting the band east of the area.\n\nNorth/northwest flow aloft will remain in place over next few days, \nkeeping temperatures cooler than normal through the beginning of \nnext week. The upper trough will slowly shift east late this weekend \nwith ridging filling in behind it. This will result in the rising of \nmid-level heights and temperatures gradually warming by the middle \nto late parts of next week. Surface high pressure will keep \nprecipitation chances minimal this weekend into next week.",
